     "text": "**CLINICAL SUMMARY - Brown Walter and James Medical Group**\n\n**Patient:** Darrell Taylor \n**DOB:** 03/29/1985 \n**Sex:** M \n**MRN:** 881039\n\n**Encounters:**\n\n**2024-12-16** \n- **Reason for Visit:** Rash \n- **BP:** 108/93 \n- **Weight:** 120 lb \nThe patient presented with a rash, which appears to related to an allergic reaction. No other abnormalities were noted during the physical examination.\n\n**2024-05-29** \n- **Reason for Visit:** Suicide attempt \n- **BP:** 113/83 \n- **Weight:** 126 lb \nThe patient was seen following a suicide attempt. He reported feeling overwhelmed by personal stressors. Cognitive-behavioral therapy was recommended, and he is currently on no medications for this issue. \n\n**2023-11-09** \n- **Reason for Visit:** Suicide attempt \n- **BP:** 111/84 \n- **Weight:** 127 lb \nThis visit followed another suicide attempt. The patient expressed feelings of hopelessness and social isolation. Continued therapy sessions were recommended, and the patient was advised to avoid isolation.\n\n**2023-04-22** \n- **Reason for Visit:** Medication refill \n- **BP:** 114/93 \n- **Weight:** 112 lb \nThe patient came in for a routine medication refill. Blood pressure and weight were within normal limits. No new issues were reported.\n\n**2022-10-02** \n- **Reason for Visit:** Suicide attempt \n- **BP:** 120/87 \n- **Weight:** 126 lb \nThe patient reported another suicide attempt linked to ongoing depressive symptoms. Therapy sessions were intensified, and he was advised to establish a stronger support network.\n\n**2022-03-15** \n- **Reason for Visit:** Suicide attempt \n- **BP:** 113/91 \n- 116 lb \nThis visit occurred after a suicide attempt. The patient was experiencing severe depressive episodes. Anxiolytic medications were briefly considered but not prescribed.\n\n**2021-08-25** \n- **Reason for Visit:** Suicide attempt \n- **BP:** 112/90 \n- **Weight:** 127 lb \nThe patient reported a suicide attempt following a stressful life event. He was referred to a mental health specialist for intensive therapy. There were no medications prescribed for this issue at the time.\n\n**2021-02-05** \n- **Reason for Visit:** Suicide attempt \n- **BP:** 122/93 \n- **Weight:** 123 lb \nThe patient came in following a suicide attempt. He reported feeling extremely hopeless and having thoughts of self-harm. Emergency psychiatric intervention was provided, and the patient was referred to ongoing psychological support.\n\n**Assessment/Plan:**\n\n- **Suicide attempt (3.8 years ago) - controlled; meds: none** \nThe patient has a history of multiple suicide attem"
    },
    {
     "page": 2,
     "text": "pts, the most recent being 3.8 years ago. Currently, the situation is under control, and no medications have been prescribed for this condition. The patient is engaged in regular cognitive-behavioral therapy sessions to address underlying mental health issues.\n\n- **Social History:** \n - **Tobacco use - cigar current 8/yr** \n The patient reports using cigars approximately eight times a year, starting from July 2007. He has been honest about his tobacco use and has not attempted to quit.\n \n - **Alcohol use - ~3 drinks/week** \n The patient consumes approximately three alcoholic drinks per week.\n\n**Additional Notes:**\n\nThroughout the visits, the patient's blood pressure and weight remained relatively stable, with no significant abnormalities in physical examinations. Vital signs and weight are as follows:\n\n- **2024-12-16:** BP 108/93, Weight 120 lb **2024-05-29:** BP 113/83, Weight 126 lb \n- **2023-11-09:** BP 111/84, Weight 127 lb \n- **2023-04-22:** BP 114/93, Weight 112 lb \n- **2022-10-02:** BP 120/87, Weight 126 lb \n- **2022-03-15:** BP 113/91, Weight 116 lb \n- **2021-08-25:** BP 112/90, Weight 127 lb \n- **2021-02-05:** BP 122/93, Weight 123 lb \n\n**Conclusion:**\n\nDarrell Taylor has a well-documented history of suicide attempts, the most recent being 3.8 years ago. His current mental health status is under control, and he is receiving ongoing cognitive-behavioral therapy. There are no medications prescribed for his history of suicide attempts. His blood pressure and weight remain within normal limits during routine visits, and no other significant medical issues have been noted. His social history includes cigar use and moderate alcohol consumption.\n"
